    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f83ce831_4d3c7013",
        "filename": "services/core/java/com/android/server/ConnectivityService.java",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 6163,
      "author": {
        "id": 1015428
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-12-22T12:05:07Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Is there any way to identify that the NA info is indeed coming from such an authenticated source ? I\u0027m a bit worried that future uses from NetworkAgents might not share this property but will still be prioritized.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 6163,
        "startChar": 11,
        "endLine": 6163,
        "endChar": 52
      },
      "revId": "01f3d2b03e4a7867028da02dd8dbbe002212a599",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a61d9b38_7f55d0d4",
        "filename": "services/core/java/com/android/server/ConnectivityService.java",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 6163,
      "author": {
        "id": 1480931
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-12-23T01:24:05Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m not sure what the AI is here for me. My Passpoint implementation will retrieve the venue URL through PMF enabled ANQP exchange (PMF \u003d Protected Management Frames). So it is guaranteed by the link layer that this are trusted comparing to capport that is transmitted in the clear without any protection in the link layer, which can probably be spoofed by an attacker.",
      "parentUuid": "f83ce831_4d3c7013",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 6163,
        "startChar": 11,
        "endLine": 6163,
        "endChar": 52
      },
      "revId": "01f3d2b03e4a7867028da02dd8dbbe002212a599",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "981efdb5_e819d9dc",
        "filename": "services/core/java/com/android/server/ConnectivityService.java",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 6163,
      "author": {
        "id": 1015428
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-12-24T01:56:43Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This is a general API with comments assuming only one caller deep in the stack, and deriving security assumptions from them, and this is historically a dangerous thing to do. So I was hinting at some way for the code in CS to know whether the venue URL from the NA has indeed been retrieved from such a secure source. Passpoint is such a source, but here you\u0027re building a general API, and the comments just assume this info is coming from Passpoint and has the security guarantees.\n\nBut any NA can call this API, and as soon as a non-Passpoint NA calls this API, the comments you left suddenly become incorrect. Whether that new non-Passpoint NA also provides similar guarantees is anybody\u0027s guess, and so I was wondering whether we needed to tell CS that this information is indeed subject to the security guarantees you describe in comments.\n\nThis is especially relevant as while this code becomes Mainline, NetworkAgent isn\u0027t. The code here means that, e.g., an OEM where WiFi is optional can trivially send whatever venue info URL they want and have it override the Capport one, including by mistake. Or they could do this on CBRS. I am concerned about this.\n\nDoes this clarify my comment ?",
      "parentUuid": "a61d9b38_7f55d0d4",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 6163,
        "startChar": 11,
        "endLine": 6163,
        "endChar": 52
      },
      "revId": "01f3d2b03e4a7867028da02dd8dbbe002212a599",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9b2117e8_750b4a35",
        "filename": "services/core/java/com/android/server/ConnectivityService.java",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 6163,
      "author": {
        "id": 1480931
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-12-28T21:48:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Understood, but not clear to me how to resolve it in a way that satisfies your requirements.\nOne one hand - Passpoint is more secure that Capport and should be given prioritization. It is the preferred solution to replace the insecure captive portals and provides venue URL and T\u0026C support in-band.\nOn the other hand - what you mentioned.\n\nIs there a permission mechanism that can be used here?",
      "parentUuid": "981efdb5_e819d9dc",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 6163,
        "startChar": 11,
        "endLine": 6163,
        "endChar": 52
      },
      "revId": "01f3d2b03e4a7867028da02dd8dbbe002212a599",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c2d2f184_123ec232",
        "filename": "services/core/java/com/android/server/ConnectivityService.java",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 6163,
      "author": {
        "id": 1288034
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-01-06T09:16:49Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think a first step could be to remove all references to \"passpoint\" in the comments here and make them more generic, since there is no guarantee that the info given by the NetworkAgent comes from Passpoint (the most simple case being another network agent adding a feature to receive captive portal data from another source).\n\nI don\u0027t see much more we could do to ensure OEMs don\u0027t send bad captive portal data by mistake though - apart from prioritizing capport API data when its there instead of network agent data maybe, but I can see that it\u0027s not necessarily the right call for passpoint since the capport API URL is not sent securely.",
      "parentUuid": "9b2117e8_750b4a35",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 6163,
        "startChar": 11,
        "endLine": 6163,
        "endChar": 52
      },
      "revId": "01f3d2b03e4a7867028da02dd8dbbe002212a599",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e928cb95_a27135d0",
        "filename": "services/core/java/com/android/server/ConnectivityService.java",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 6163,
      "author": {
        "id": 1015428
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-01-06T12:10:15Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "One thing we could do is to have some metadata saying whether this information is authenticated. Passpoint would say yes, Capport would say no, and we\u0027d prioritize the authenticated one. I\u0027m not sure this is very future-proof though.\n\nWe can do something a little bit more ad-hoc but that will serve us well : have an enum for the source of information, including UNKNOWN or OTHER. So we\u0027d know this information is coming from Capport or from Passpoint, and we\u0027d only prioritize the info from the NA over Capport if it\u0027s coming from Passpoint. It may also prove useful for debugging, and it lets us implement arbitrarily complex logic depending on the source if we\u0027re in a hurry.\n\nWDYT ?",
      "parentUuid": "c2d2f184_123ec232",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 6163,
        "startChar": 11,
        "endLine": 6163,
        "endChar": 52
      },
      "revId": "01f3d2b03e4a7867028da02dd8dbbe002212a599",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ff180f72_8314d861",
        "filename": "services/core/java/com/android/server/ConnectivityService.java",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 6163,
      "author": {
        "id": 1288034
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-01-06T12:21:44Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "If we put such an enum in CaptivePortalData I think it should be a bit field, since the output capport data here is merging info from both.\nThis may be a bit complicated to implement, so I\u0027m wondering if it may be better done in a change on top.",
      "parentUuid": "e928cb95_a27135d0",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 6163,
        "startChar": 11,
        "endLine": 6163,
        "endChar": 52
      },
      "revId": "01f3d2b03e4a7867028da02dd8dbbe002212a599",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e651e11d_4d9f4773",
        "filename": "services/core/java/com/android/server/ConnectivityService.java",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 6163,
      "author": {
        "id": 1480931
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-01-07T02:19:04Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Done removing all references to Passpoint.\n\n+1 to do it on top, it will allow me to finalize the feature. \nNote that it will not prevent mistakes, or explicit use to overwrite the capport venue URL by OEMs, they will simply use the enum/bitfield value that will work for them. Perhaps the key is to identify the caller and prioritize based on that (i.e. Wifi service, comparing to an app).",
      "parentUuid": "ff180f72_8314d861",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 6163,
        "startChar": 11,
        "endLine": 6163,
        "endChar": 52
      },
      "revId": "01f3d2b03e4a7867028da02dd8dbbe002212a599",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "809dc820_0b7f44c5",
        "filename": "services/core/java/com/android/server/ConnectivityService.java",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 6163,
      "author": {
        "id": 1015428
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-01-08T10:32:47Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I agree that for simplicity such a change should implemented separately, but I don\u0027t think we can declare feature complete until it is done, because I am far too scared of this being left just unimplemented.\n\nThe more I think about it the more I think the current implementation that just assumes a given client is falling short of taking appropriate steps to prevent accidental breach of trust, where Android devices might be displaying malicious URLs resulting from this code just assuming authentication guarantees because we happen to only have one client combined with a well-intentioned implementation by an OEM that didn\u0027t know about this particular tenet. I think this is important enough to stopship.\n\nYes, identifying the caller is exactly the kind of thing I mean. I don\u0027t think it should be a bit field ; instead I suggest having an IntDef with Unknown, Mixed, Capport, Passpoint. We can add more later. Mixed would not retain what were the original components. I think it\u0027s much better to call out Passpoint rather than WiFi, as it is easy to imagine WiFi may have other sources than Passpoint in the future.",
      "parentUuid": "e651e11d_4d9f4773",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 6163,
        "startChar": 11,
        "endLine": 6163,
        "endChar": 52
      },
      "revId": "01f3d2b03e4a7867028da02dd8dbbe002212a599",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "61242ae4_d151674d",
        "filename": "services/core/java/com/android/server/ConnectivityService.java",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 6163,
      "author": {
        "id": 1480931
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-01-09T00:39:09Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027ll set up a meeting to discuss this. I don\u0027t see how an IntDef would make any difference here.\n1. IIUC, it requires a permission to modify the network stack, regular apps don\u0027t have that.\n2. If an OEM who has this permission decides to post a malicious URL, then I don\u0027t see how we can stop them because they are supposed to be trusted. They can also send it with a \"Passpoint\" value of your proposed IntDef.\n3. The Merge method has 2 very distinct sources for the data: Capport and NA. What would it mean to have a Capport or Mixed or Unknown IntDef values from the NA? It is not Capport for sure, and if NA data exists, it is already Mixed.\n\nWhat if we modify the code back to prefer Capport over NA? I don\u0027t think any Passpoint deployments will use Capport anyway, it is supposed to replace/upgrade the insecure captive portal technology.",
      "parentUuid": "809dc820_0b7f44c5",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 6163,
        "startChar": 11,
        "endLine": 6163,
        "endChar": 52
      },
      "revId": "01f3d2b03e4a7867028da02dd8dbbe002212a599",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
